</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">email: </span><a href="mailto:xiangzheng.lxz@alibaba-inc.com">xiangzheng.lxz@alibaba-inc.com</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">© acmlicensed</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Click-through rate (CTR) prediction plays an important role in online advertising systems. On the one hand, traditional CTR prediction models capture the collaborative signals in tabular data via feature interaction modeling, but they lose semantics in text. On the other hand, Large Language Models (LLMs) excel in understanding the context and meaning behind text, but they face challenges in capturing collaborative signals and they have long inference latency. In this paper, we aim to leverage the benefits of both types of models and pursue collaboration, semantics and efficiency. We present ELEC, which is an <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">E</span>fficient <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">L</span>LM-<span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">E</span>mpowered <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">C</span>TR prediction framework. We first adapt an LLM for the CTR prediction task. In order to leverage the ability of the LLM but simultaneously keep efficiency, we utilize the pseudo-siamese network which contains a gain network and a vanilla network. We inject the high-level representation vector generated by the LLM into a collaborative CTR model to form the gain network such that it can take advantage of both tabular modeling and textual modeling. However, its reliance on the LLM limits its efficiency. We then distill the knowledge from the gain network to the vanilla network on both the score level and the representation level, such that the vanilla network takes only tabular data as input, but can still generate comparable performance as the gain network. Our approach is model-agnostic. It allows for the integration with various existing LLMs and collaborative CTR models. Experiments on real-world datasets demonstrate the effectiveness and efficiency of ELEC for CTR prediction.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Online advertising; Click-through rate (CTR) prediction; Large Language Model-empowered CTR prediction
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Click-through rate (CTR) prediction is one of the most central tasks in online advertising systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shan et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qu et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
It aims to predict the probability that a user will click on a specific ad.
Traditional CTR prediction models heavily depend on tabular data (e.g., categorical features) and they capture the collaborative signals via feature interaction modeling, e.g., FM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rendle, 2010</a>)</cite>, DeepFM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, AutoInt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, DCN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2017</a>)</cite> and DCNv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. However, they cannot effectively process textual information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, the emergence of Large Language Models (LLMs) has given rise to another possibility for CTR prediction, which takes text as input. LLMs excel in understanding the context and meaning behind text, but they face challenges in capturing collaborative signals. In the recommendation domain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, language-based models show effectiveness in scenarios with limited user-item interactions, but they are inferior to traditional collaborative models when abundant user-item interactions are available <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Moreover, LLMs suffer from low efficiency in online inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we aim to leverage the benefits of both types of models and pursue collaboration, semantics and efficiency. We present ELEC, which is an <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">E</span>fficient <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">L</span>LM-<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">E</span>mpowered <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">C</span>TR prediction framework. In order to tailor an LLM for CTR prediction, we add a transformation layer and a prediction layer, and train the newly added parameters on CTR data.
In order to leverage the ability of the LLM but maintain efficiency at the same time, we utilize the pseudo-siamese network which contains a gain network and a vanilla network. We inject the high-level representation vector generated by the LLM into a collaborative model to form the gain network such that it can benefit from both tabular modeling and textual modeling. However, the gain network is not efficient. We then distill the knowledge from the gain network to the vanilla network such that the latter takes only tabular data as input, but can still generate comparable performance as the former.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The main contributions of this paper are</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose ELEC for CTR prediction. ELEC leverages the benefits of both collaborative models and language models. It thus captures both collaborative and semantic signals.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ELEC is efficient. Its online part does not depend on LLMs and eliminates the long inference latency by LLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ELEC is also model-agnostic. It allows the integration of various existing LLMs and collaborative CTR prediction models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments on real-world datasets to evaluate the performance of various types of methods.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07594v1/model.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/181;" width="476" height="181" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>ELEC framework. (a) Tabular data to textual data. (b) Modifying an LLM for CTR prediction (Trans. - Transformation; Rep. - Representation). (c) Integrating the modified LLM with the collaborative CTR model. For simplicity, the aggregation layer in the gain network uses vector concatenation. Only the vanilla network is used for online inference.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Model Design</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Background</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the CTR dataset as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{t}_{i},y_{i})}_{i=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{t}_{i},y_{i})}_{i=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is the sample index, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is tabular data, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is textual data and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the label indicating a user’s actual click behavior. The goal of CTR prediction is to estimate the click probability based on the given input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional CTR prediction models take tabular data <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as input and generate a probability in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> as output.
LLMs are not designed for the CTR prediction task, they can take textual data <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as input but they generate text as output.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to leverage the benefits of both types of models and simultaneously keep the model efficient for CTR prediction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Modifying an LLM for CTR Prediction</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs provide a potential way for CTR prediction by leveraging their powerful knowledge and textual reasoning ability.
However, LLMs are not initially designed for CTR prediction.
We make the following three revisions in order to adapt an LLM for CTR prediction.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.1. </span><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input: from tabular data to textual data</span></h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The input for CTR prediction consists of tabular data (e.g., categorical features) that cannot be directly processed by LLMs.
Therefore, we first transform tabular data into textual data. We adopt a simple yet effective transformation template <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which is the concatenation of “&lt;<span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">field_name</span>&gt; is &lt;<span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">feature_value</span>&gt;” (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ ELEC: Efficient Large Language Model-Empowered Click-Through Rate Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a)).
For example, if the original tabular data <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is “gender: female, occupation: college student”, then the transformed text is “Gender is female. Occupation is college student.”
Moreover, other text information <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such as long descriptions (optional) that are not in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can also be fed to the LLM. To reduce noise in long text, we can use any LLM to summarize the long text into tags. All such text information forms the input <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the LLM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.2. </span><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output: adding a transformation layer and a prediction layer</span></h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the set of hidden states from the last layer of the LLM, we take the <span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">average pooling</span> of these hidden states as the initial semantic information representation.
We then add a transformation layer to transform this representation from the semantic space to the CTR prediction space (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ ELEC: Efficient Large Language Model-Empowered Click-Through Rate Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b)). For simplicity, the transformation layer consists of an MLP with several fully connected layers. We then obtain a high-level representation vector.
Finally, a prediction layer with the sigmoid activation function is used to output the CTR prediction (i.e., a value in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.3. </span><span id="S2.SS2.SSS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training: training newly added model parameters</span></h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We keep the original model parameters of the LLM frozen and only train the newly added model parameters in the transformation layer and the prediction layer using CTR data. In this way, the modified LLM is adapted to the CTR prediction task.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Integrating the Modified LLM and the Traditional Collaborative CTR Model</h3>

<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.1. </span><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Design Objectives</span></h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like the target model to be both effective and efficient. By effective, we mean that the target model should integrate the strength from both the modified LLM and the traditional collaborative CTR model. By efficient, we mean that the target model should have short online inference time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.2. </span><span id="S2.SS3.SSS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pseudo-siamese Network</span></h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We leverage the pseudo-siamese network to achieve both effectiveness and efficiency. The pseudo-siamese network offers more flexibility than the siamese network, as it allows two branches to have different structures and to receive inputs from various modalities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.
In particular, our pseudo-siamese network consists of a gain network and a vanilla network.
The gain network has a modified LLM module (all parameters are frozen) and a collaborative module, while the vanilla network has only a collaborative module (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ ELEC: Efficient Large Language Model-Empowered Click-Through Rate Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(c)).
The collaborative modules of the gain network and the vanilla network have the same structure, e.g., both are DNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2016</a>)</cite> or DCNv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
Generally, collaborative CTR models adopt the “Embedding &amp; Feature Interaction” paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
The input to the gain network contains both textual data <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and tabular data <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the input to the vanilla network contains only tabular data <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.3. </span><span id="S2.SS3.SSS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model training and knowledge distillation</span></h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The gain network is expected to generate better performance as it can process both textual data and tabular data. However, it is not efficient.
The vanilla network is efficient by design, but it can process only tabular data.
Therefore, we would like to transfer the knowledge from the gain network to the vanilla network.
In this way, the vanilla network achieves both effectiveness and efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We perform knowledge distillation at two levels: <span id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">score level</span> (coarse-grained scalar) and <span id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">representation level</span> (fine-grained vector). At the score level, we would like the vanilla network not only mimics the point-wise calibration ability of the gain network, but also the list-wise ranking ability. Therefore, we utilize the Calibration-compatible LIstwise Distillation (CLID) loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gui et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to achieve this goal. It is given by</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L_{score}=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}Q_{g,i}\log Q_{v,i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mi>Q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>Q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{score}=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}Q_{g,i}\log Q_{v,i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{g,i}=\frac{p_{g,i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}p_{g,j}},\ \ Q_{v,i}=\frac{p_{v,i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}p_{v,j}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{g,i}=\frac{p_{g,i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}p_{g,j}},\ \ Q_{v,i}=\frac{p_{v,i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}p_{v,j}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is the sample index, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of samples in a mini-batch, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g,i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the estimated probability by the gain network and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v,i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the estimated probability by the vanilla network.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At the representation level, we would like the high-level representation vector <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{v,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{v,i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the vanilla network to be similar to <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{g,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{g,i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the gain network (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ ELEC: Efficient Large Language Model-Empowered Click-Through Rate Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(c)).
In particular, we use the mean squared error (MSE) loss to achieve this goal</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{rep}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|\mathbf{h}_{v,i}-\mathbf{h}_{g,i}\right\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{rep}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left\|\mathbf{h}_{v,i}-\mathbf{h}_{g,i}\right\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, both networks use the label information <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to guide the training and optimize the binary cross-entropy loss</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{gain}=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(y_{i}\log p_{g,i}+(1-y_{i})\log(1-p_{g,i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{gain}=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(y_{i}\log p_{g,i}+(1-y_{i})\log(1-p_{g,i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{van}=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(y_{i}\log p_{v,i}+(1-y_{i})\log(1-p_{v,i})\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{van}=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(y_{i}\log p_{v,i}+(1-y_{i})\log(1-p_{v,i})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we train the gain network and the vanilla network jointly</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{total}=L_{gain}+L_{van}+L_{score}+\alpha L_{rep}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{total}=L_{gain}+L_{van}+L_{score}+\alpha L_{rep},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a balancing hyperparameter. Only collaborative modules are trained. <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{score}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{score}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{rep}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{rep}</annotation></semantics></math> only impact the vanilla network.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.4. </span><span id="S2.SS3.SSS4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Online Inference</span></h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the vanilla network for online inference, which takes only tabular data as input.
As the performance of the vanilla network is similar to that of the gain network, but the vanilla network does not depend on the LLM and has only a collaborative module, it achieves both effectiveness and efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Datasets</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The statistics of the experimental datasets are listed in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1. Datasets ‣ 3. Experiments ‣ ELEC: Efficient Large Language Model-Empowered Click-Through Rate Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">1) <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Amazon dataset</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We use a subset “All Beauty”. We convert ratings that are greater than 3 to label 1 and others to label 0.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">2) <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Industry dataset</span>. It is sampled from the click log of an industrial news feed advertising platform.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Statistics of experimental datasets.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
